 There is no file, socket, process or raw-input interface, on purpose.
 
+### Mod channels (experimental, API level 8)
+
+`capabilities = ["net"]` and `experimental = ["net"]` enable a private channel
+between a client and server component with the same mod id. The Rust SDK exposes
+`net` on both sides. `connected()` reports server support to the client;
+`send(None, bytes)` sends to its server, while a server uses
+`send(Some(player_uuid), bytes)`. Server manifests still declare `players` for
+the player types in their exported callbacks.
+
+`poll()` returns `received` and client `disconnected` events. Received server
+messages include the authenticated player's UUID and a host-generated session
+token; clients cannot choose either. `session-token(player)` gives the current
+token on the server. If a mod queues messages itself, compare the captured token
+against this value before handling them. Reconnecting with the same player UUID
+rotates the token. The transport binds queued replies to the actual connection
+generation so a late reply cannot reach a replacement connection.
+
+The game uses `horizon:mod` custom payloads over the existing connection. Nothing
+is sent on a vanilla server. In-frame single player uses the same inboxes as a
+loopback; the integrated server, LAN and dedicated server use the normal packet
+handlers. Separate client/server `.hmod` files may share an id; two running
+components on the same side may not. Only one package should own any content
+namespace.
+
+Messages are at most 16 KiB, each hook can send 8, and each inbox holds at most
+64 events / 256 KiB. Full inboxes refuse new messages. Mods need acknowledgements
+and application limits appropriate to their gameplay. Disconnect removes stale
+traffic; a trapped hook's network outputs are discarded. This capability grants
+no sockets, world writes, inventory access or damage authority.
+
